New item: Metal detector

Implementation details: they would have charges the same way brass
lanterns have charges, using one per turn in use, and at about the
same number.  They'd be charged by scrolls of charging and the
Platinum Yendorian Express Card.  They would automatically be "on"
while wielded, and off when unwielded, and consume energy out of its
charge pool so long as they are held in hands.  A metal detector out
of charges does nothing when wielded.  (It may or may not be obvious
this has happened.)

The primary use for metal detectors would be the detection of buried
items.  Thus, part of this idea is the addition of buried objects in
the game's random item generation and special level code (if it's not
there already).  Only metal buried items are found (other buried items
could still be found by Detection scrolls and spells).

Since the metal-detecting head of such a device is oriented towards
the ground and skims the floor, metal detectors would be more likely
to find something buried than something underground than something on
the floor nearby or encased in rock nearby.  However, they still
detect these things.

When wielded, if the player is within five spaces of a buried metal
object, a metal detector would beep according to proximity.  One beep
per two turns: two to five spaces away from a buried metal object. 
One beep per turn: next to such an object.  Rapid beeping: standing on
one.

If the player is standing one space from an unburied metal object, or
one is in the wall nearby, it'd beep once per two turns.  If multiple
sources are triggering a detector, it reports the "highest" level of
activity.


If the player is carrying or wearing metal: since the metal-detecting
part of the device is on the ground, these objects do not trigger the
detector unless they're worn on the feet.  So wearing iron (or random
metal) boots would always cause the detector to beep once every two
turns, unless standing next to or on top of a buried object.


Lodestones would cause a metal detector to go wild (is this behavior
accurate of powerful magnets and RL detectors?), and are detected from
much farther away.  (Details not cooked up yet.)


Other uses:
For people who don't know about the kick test, or can't use it because
the item is in a chest or surrounded by undiggable walls (such as in
some mineend configurations), this could be a useful way to ID
lodestones.

Metal detectors would also aid in the discovery of traps with metal
components, including and especailly the usable types, bear traps and
land mines.  Darts have metal tips and would be detected but arrow
traps would not.

Monsters with a metalic component, like iron golems, would also set it
off.


The more I think about it, the more interesting it seems to me.  What
do you guys think?

